FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1717 — A `to` declared anywhere beneath a module’s body is attributed to the module with `frames.last().and_then(|frame| frame.module)`, but nested block frames are opened via `Frame::scoped(FrameKind::If)` at src/lsp.rs:1481 (and similarly for `unless`/loops) and are never given that module binding. The runtime only publishes a module’s *top-level* `to` items (a `to` inside an `if` is not a `module` body item), so either (a) if block frames do inherit `module`, a nested function is wrongly recorded as a published member at src/lsp.rs:1746-1748, and it additionally removes a same-named published value at src/lsp.rs:1755-1756; or (b) if they do not inherit it, the round-3 claim that a nested-branch `set` navigates cannot hold (see finding 2). Either way the module-owner resolution for `to` is wrong; it must require the declaration frame to be the `Module` frame itself rather than `frames.last()`. Settle it by checking `Frame::scoped`’s `module` initializer and running a fixture with `module M / if ... then to f() ... end / export all / end` followed by `M.f()` — the function must not resolve. VERIFIED: traced the exact path (src/lsp.rs:1717 → 1746-1757, frame opened at src/lsp.rs:1481 with no `.module` assignment, unlike src/lsp.rs:1609).

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1449-1454 — Publication of module `set` values is filtered against `members`, which comes from `module_declared`; `module_declared` is only appended when `top_module = frames.last().and_then(|frame| frame.module)` is `Some` (src/lsp.rs:1930,1940-1945). But the value binding itself is recorded using the `runtime_module` walk (`frames.iter().rev().find(pushes_runtime_scope)`, src/lsp.rs:1935-1939,1955-1963). For a `set` inside an `if`/`unless`/loop in a module body these two notions disagree: the value lands in `module_values`, yet the name is absent from `module_declared`, so `declared_module_values` drops it at module close and `Module.name` answers `None`. This is exactly the nested-branch case the round-3 report says it fixed; the same split also makes the published-function removal at src/lsp.rs:1955 and the value replacement at src/lsp.rs:1755 resolve different owners. Fix: derive the owning module once (the innermost runtime frame for `set` values/writes) and use that same owner when appending to `module_declared`, so the publication filter and the value table agree. Settle it by running the added nested-branch `set` fixture and asserting a bare `M.f` resolves, plus a module `constant`-after-`set` fixture. VERIFIED: traced src/lsp.rs:1930/1940-1945 against the `runtime_module` used at src/lsp.rs:1935-1939 and the filter at src/lsp.rs:1449-1454.

